  "sistemaId": "-KPZgfxIjCFs_05eB3oM",
  "sistemaWhy": "The map holds two networks: TA TICH (north) and Nohoch Aktun (south, with the Miss Aktun of the caption). The database has both sistemas, Tatich (-KPZgfyMtMazy0q2zOdT, aka Nuhuch Actun) and Nohoch Aktun (-KPZgfxIjCFs_05eB3oM), and both list this map (-P2lcvM-PLnOl9aSWD1k). Only one sistemaId fits a config: Nohoch Aktun, after the config's name.",
  "utmEpsg": 32616,
  "placementWhy": "Weak: one control point plus the scale bar and the north arrow. The jungle canopy hides every collapse on both Esri and Google imagery (z18), and OSM has nothing there. The one point is the Ta Tich collapse (the yellow Effondrement west of the main gallery): the database's Cenote Nohoch Aktun (Gerrard: Sistema Tatich, 20°26.201'N 87°20.805'W) sits exactly at the end of a footpath visible on both imageries, which loops in from the dirt road to the south-west. Scale from the bar (0-500 m, 573 px: 0.873 m/px), north up as the arrow. Unchecked: the same publication's Xel-Ha map came out about 10 degrees off its north arrow; Gerrard's Sistema Nohoch Aktun position (20°25.652'N 87°20.782'W) falls 270 m west of the nearest traced passage and 360 m from the nearest collapse with this placement: either Gerrard's point is rough, or the map is turned (a 20-25 degree clockwise turn would bring the Siphon 800 m passage onto it), which nothing on the ground can confirm.",
